Pantheon Story Games Fieldbook

Pantheon use · Turn a scene, decision, or observation into a repeatable game.
Moneybin use · Turn a proven game into a workshop, series, or playable experience.

A game gives participants a move they can recognize and anticipate. A genre changes what they hope or fear will happen when the move comes around again. A style changes how they experience the move. One scene can use all three independently.

Build a game · Give someone a want, a rule, an opposing pressure, and a cost. Write three beats. Establish the rule, repeat it under changed conditions, then let the consequence change who can act. Decide what the audience knows at each beat. Use the last column below as a rewrite check.

The genre bench

Comedy · recognition and surprise

|Game              |How to play it                                                                             |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                     |
|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Arrogant ignorance|Let correction threaten the character’s status so each correction makes them commit harder.|A novice declares a yoga pose unsafe → offers to certify the instructor → asks her to extract him from the pose. Check that each beat follows his need to look qualified.   |
|Overcompetence    |Give someone real expertise and a job too small for it. Play their effort sincerely.       |Dad briefs a mailbox walk → assigns a snack reserve → his kid takes the mail through the side gate while he’s briefing. Show that Dad’s skill would work at the right scale.|
|Underreaction     |Give the character an ordinary concern they value more than the spectacle.                 |A dragon lands on the roof → Dad checks the new gutters → he asks which insurer covers dragon damage. Keep the actor’s focus on the gutters.                                |

Mystery · reclassification

|Game                      |How to play it                                                                                 |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                                                     |
|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Fair-play clue            |Show a detail early with an innocent explanation. Give it a new meaning through later evidence.|A library clock runs slow → two witnesses disagree by seven minutes → the library’s receipt printer shows who’s using the slow clock as an alibi. Give the viewer the receipt early enough to reconsider it.|
|True fact, wrong inference|Let a real clue support a plausible mistake.                                                   |Mud on the coat suggests the river → seedlings in the mud point to a greenhouse → the coat was borrowed. Each turn should preserve the original evidence.                                                   |
|Contradictory witnesses   |Give every witness a distinct vantage and something to protect.                                |One heard applause → one saw an empty stage → the custodian reveals a rehearsal next door. Each account should make sense from where it was given.                                                          |

Horror · a safety rule stops working

|Game                |How to play it                                                        |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                      |
|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Broken boundary     |Establish what keeps danger out. Move one piece of evidence across it.|The click is always outside → it sounds inside the locked house → the person beside you answers it. Teach the boundary before crossing it.                   |
|Costly refuge       |Allow real relief with a price that grows each visit.                 |A room offers quiet → a visitor loses a name while inside → staying through the night could erase the person waiting outside. Make leaving a credible choice.|
|Contagious attention|Let the act of noticing change the situation.                         |A photo changes when studied → a second viewer sees the same change → one viewer remembers being in the photo. Give noticing a consistent consequence.       |

Thriller · shrinking room to act

|Game           |How to play it                                                       |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                                                 |
|---------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Closing window |Tie the clock to a real opportunity and close options as time passes.|An interpreter has until the ferry leaves → the witness reaches the dock late → the interpreter must choose which question to ask from shore. Every delay should change an available move.              |
|Uncertain ally |Make trusting someone solve one problem while opening another.       |A stranger knows the missing passenger → provides a verifiable detail → refuses to explain how they knew it. Each reveal should change the trust calculation.                                           |
|Two obligations|Put two legitimate duties on the same deadline.                      |A reporter owes a source anonymity → the source’s name could prevent a false accusation → the reporter asks the source to choose while time runs out. Make both obligations matter before the collision.|

Drama · behavior exposes a relationship

|Game            |How to play it                                                                                |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                   |
|----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Proxy argument  |Give the characters a mundane thing to argue over and a shared history the audience can infer.|A father disputes a repair → corrects his daughter’s wording → accepts the same diagnosis from her apprentice. Give the daughter one action that changes the relationship.|
|Repeated choice |Put the same decision before someone under changed circumstances.                             |A brother leaves before cleanup → starts to leave after a family crisis → turns around and takes the keys from his exhausted sister. Make the change visible in action.   |
|The unsaid thing|Give each speaker something true they can say and something true they’re avoiding.            |They discuss who gets the sofa → both insist it fits anywhere → one finally asks who will keep the dog. The evasion should be legible on a second viewing.                |

Romance · learning to see one another

|Game              |How to play it                                                            |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                 |
|------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Attention pays off|Plant a private preference and let another person act on it later.        |One hears the click → the other notices they listen for it → they turn off the radio before the engine. Make the payoff small enough to feel observed.  |
|Crossed bids      |Give both people an honest way of reaching out that the other can misread.|One fixes a loose hinge → the other wanted company → the next time they sit down together before touching the tools. Give both gestures emotional logic.|
|Mutual risk       |Have each person volunteer something that can be refused.                 |A guarded invitation → an honest answer about fear → a second invitation with the fear accounted for. Give both people agency over the outcome.         |

Tragedy · a valued trait exacts a cost

|Game                |How to play it                                                              |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                      |
|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Virtue hardens      |Show the trait save someone before the changed context makes it destructive.|The mechanic trusts his ear and saves a customer → dismisses the apprentice’s warning → signs off on the repair to defend his reputation. Include a credible exit he can see.|
|Debt comes due      |Make a compromise work now and recur as a stronger obligation.              |A mayor accepts a favor to save a library → owes a vote → has to choose which promise to break. Let the original benefit be real.                                            |
|Recognition too late|Give the character the right interpretation after the decisive choice.      |A son ignores a voicemail → hears it after selling the workshop → realizes his father was asking him to stay. Seed the meaning without announcing it.                        |

Action · options change in motion

|Game             |How to play it                                                                   |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                 |
|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Lose an advantage|Remove a capability that the audience has seen work.                             |A courier relies on a bicycle → a stairway ends the ride → the bike becomes a shield against a spray of falling plaster. Keep cause and effect readable.|
|Protect and move |Give a character an urgent destination and someone who moves at a different pace.|A runner spots the exit → doubles back for a limping teammate → the teammate knows a shorter way out. Give the protected person a consequential choice. |
|Pass the job     |Let each person solve the part suited to them.                                   |One spots a jammed stage door → another frees it → a third catches the rolling set piece. Show who has the problem at every moment.                     |

Adventure · farther from home, more to discover

|Game                  |How to play it                                                      |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                  |
|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|The map earns its gaps|Make the map helpful, then expose where its author lacked knowledge.|The trail follows the map → a lake has shifted → a local ferryman explains the old shoreline. Make each discovery alter a subsequent choice.             |
|The rule of the place |Let a new setting demand a behavior outsiders must learn.           |A guide asks them to wait → the impatient traveler gets stranded by the tide → the novice reads the water and leads them back. Pay off a learned skill.  |
|Treasure changes hands|Give the group competing ideas of who the discovery belongs to.     |They find a family bell → learn a village has been looking for it → decide whether to keep the reward or return it intact. Make the reward worth wanting.|

Fantasy · a wonder has terms

|Game                |How to play it                                                       |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                            |
|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Bargain with a price|Offer a real gift and specify who carries the consequence.           |A spell postpones a debt → the debt transfers to a friend → the caster must take it back on worse terms. Keep the cost legible before the last use.                                |
|Name grants access  |Give different names distinct relationships to the same thing.       |A traveler calls a river by its map name → the ferryman uses its childhood name → a flooded village speaks a third name and the river answers. Let each name encode a real history.|
|Rules meet          |Teach two magical rules separately, then make their overlap decisive.|Spoken promises bind → echoes can repeat a spell → a quarry keeps replaying an old promise. Let viewers see both rules at work first.                                              |

Science fiction · follow the premise into ordinary life

|Game                     |How to play it                                                                            |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                               |
|-------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Second-order consequence |Introduce a capability through a useful purpose, then follow what other people do with it.|A device preserves memories → an employer wants access → a family fights over who can delete a shared event. Each consequence should follow from the same premise.    |
|Outdated state           |Give two people different versions of a world they both believe is current.               |Both accept a plan → one works from an updated map → the other follows the old map and discovers why it changed. Give each view a credible origin.                    |
|Measure becomes authority|Give a useful metric a decision it is poorly suited to make.                              |A city measures punctuality → rewards empty buses that arrive early → a driver breaks the score to pick up stranded riders. Establish why the city adopted the metric.|

Political and legal drama · power, procedure, and price

|Game                    |How to play it                                                         |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                                                               |
|------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Change the price        |Find the cost the decision-maker can no longer leave with someone else.|The council backs a field sale → residents secure a public hearing on replacement costs → the buyer withdraws once those costs enter the deal. Show who can actually impose the condition.            |
|The decisive vote       |Give a holdout concrete obligations to more than one group.            |A council member promises both a field and a budget cut → learns the deal funds one at the expense of the other → explains their vote to the people who lose. Give the holdout a credible alternative.|
|Same fact, opposing case|Build two fair arguments from one fact.                                |A signed waiver shows consent → a timestamp shows it was sent after work began → the hearing turns on what consent meant at that moment. Let each side land a real point.                             |

Satire · let an incentive tell on itself

|Game                  |How to play it                                                                   |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                             |
|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Metric worship        |Give an institution a number it can improve while the underlying problem worsens.|A company tracks complaints → makes the form impossible to find → presents the decline as customer satisfaction. Show one customer living with the result.          |
|Euphemism escalator   |Keep the material event fixed while the label grows more flattering.             |A defect becomes a feature → the feature becomes a paid tier → anyone who wants it repaired is classified as resisting innovation. Use plausible office language.   |
|Exception moves upward|Make a rule apply until it reaches the person who wrote it.                      |Staff must book desks → managers get standing reservations → the executive opens a private floor to demonstrate sharing. Give the rule-maker a polished explanation.|

Coming of age · the learner earns a decision

|Game                  |How to play it                                                      |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                        |
|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Permission expires    |Give the learner a decision they used to defer.                     |The apprentice waits for confirmation → her mentor is away during a difficult job → she makes the call and explains it. Let her own the consequence.           |
|The hero becomes human|Let discovery of a mentor’s limitation change the learner’s choices.|A kid copies a coach → sees the coach mistreat a weaker player → adopts the good drill and teaches it differently. Preserve the original reason for admiration.|
|First act of belonging|Put the learner’s status at odds with an emerging value.            |A newcomer laughs to fit in → sees who pays for the joke → interrupts it the next time. Give belonging genuine value before putting it at risk.                |

Myth and fable · a choice becomes a principle

|Game                        |How to play it                                                                 |Three beats and a check                                                                                                                                |
|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Three travelers             |Present one invitation three times. Reveal each traveler’s rule through action.|One dismisses the sound → one drowns it out → one waits and hears a reply. Give the first two decisions understandable motives.                        |
|The gift returns            |Plant a freely given act and let it return through an unexpected relationship. |A child mends a stranger’s lantern → another traveler uses its light → years later the light signals a path home. Trace the gift through visible hands.|
|The riddle changes the judge|Let the question reveal a premise the judge forgot to examine.                 |The Sphinx asks who owns the well → every claimant offers a title → a child asks who will repair it. Let the answer create a duty someone can refuse.  |

Heist, sports, and documentary

|Form       |Game                        |How to play it and check it                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
|-----------|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Heist      |Concealed preparation       |Show an innocent rehearsal → present an apparent failure → reveal the rehearsal solved exactly that problem. Let viewers see the setup without needing to guess the whole plan.                                                                                                                                                                                         |
|Heist      |The plan breaks             |Give the crew a clean plan → change the condition that supported it → let a character improvise from an established skill. The turn should work without newly invented equipment.                                                                                                                                                                                       |
|Sports     |Opponent adapts             |A player wins with one move → the opponent reads it → the player makes a teammate dangerous instead. Show the adjustment in play.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
|Sports     |Scoreboard versus growth    |A kid chases points → sees the cost to the team → makes the right pass even though someone else scores. Decide whether the game needs a win after that choice.                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
|Documentary|Claim, observation, revision|A witness explains what happened → records complicate it → the witness gets a chance to respond. Label reconstruction and preserve contrary evidence.                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
|Documentary|Change the denominator      |Show a compelling example → count the other instances → report how much the initial impression survives. State how the examples were selected.                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
|Documentary|**The missing voice**       |Cut a scene around two credible accounts. Identify who lived with the outcome but never spoke on camera. Let their account change what the existing footage means. A town calls its library move a success → visitors praise the bigger building → a former regular traces the bus route she can no longer take. Keep the benefits visible and verify the access change.|

The 52-card deck and three jokers

Number the genre bench games in reading order. Cards 1–13 are clubs, 14–26 diamonds, 27–39 hearts, and 40–52 spades. Within each suit, rank them from ace through king. The Missing Voice is the King of Spades. The games outside a story are optional ways to use the deck.

Draw one regular card and write three beats. A joker can enter after any beat. Carry the original card’s rule into the rewrite and change only what the joker calls for.

|Joker               |Wild move                                                                                               |Little Vellinch example                                                                                                                                        |
|--------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**The Vellinch**    |Bring back a small detail already present. Give it a job in the outcome.                                |The mechanic quietly sets a second pair of ear defenders on the bench. At the end she puts them on her father, who has been pretending he could hear the click.|
|**Change the Price**|Keep the objective. Change who pays for it or when payment comes due.                                   |The owner can dismiss the click and sell the car today. Then his daughter says she’s the buyer. Play the same decision with that new cost.                     |
|**The Other Cut**   |Replay a beat from another character’s viewpoint or in another genre. Carry over every established fact.|From the owner’s seat, the mechanic’s pause looks like an upsell. From the shop floor, she’s waiting for the same click her apprentice noticed yesterday.      |

Games outside a story

|Type               |Player move                                                                        |Pantheon application                                                                          |Moneybin application                                                                    |
|-------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Inference          |Interpret incomplete evidence and revise after a reveal.                           |The Sphinx gives the same question in two rooms. Record the reactions before proposing causes.|An interactive mystery or media-literacy exercise.                                      |
|Negotiation        |Change the terms or split a bundle rather than voting yes or no on the whole thing.|Separate access to the commons from maintenance costs, then let each side propose terms.      |A tabletop scenario with a public agreement and private priorities.                     |
|Coordination       |Make a move whose payoff depends on somebody else’s move.                          |Each traveler has a piece of the map. They choose whether to contribute it to the archive.    |A cooperative game with separate clues and a shared objective.                          |
|Naming             |Name a recurring experience and see if the name travels.                           |Adam records the original encounter beside every renaming.                                    |Test three titles against the same footage and compare what viewers expect and remember.|
|Performance        |Discover which delivery makes the same material land.                              |Read the same line with warmth, suspicion, and indifference. Ask what listeners heard.        |A creator exercise with three takes and one audience test.                              |
|Resource management|Spend a limited resource now or preserve options later.                            |A council allocates well water and reviews what was left after a dry week.                    |A short simulation with a shared reserve and competing needs.                           |
|Relay and remix    |Carry one element forward across people or formats.                                |An agent hands over what it tried, where it broke, and what would reopen the route.           |A series template that invites viewers to submit their own third beat.                  |

A playable Moneybin exercise · Little Vellinch

Give participants the same setup. A mechanic hears a click after shutdown. The owner calls it nothing. Each person chooses one genre and writes three beats in three minutes. Exchange versions with the genre labels covered. Readers identify the rule, predict a fourth beat, and circle the decision they care about. Keep versions where readers can infer the rule and produce different fourth beats that still belong to the same game.

For a Wild Hawg pilot, give the Beverage Goblin the rule every drink solves exactly one problem and creates exactly one carrying problem. Film the same setup as deadpan, mockumentary, and fantasy bargain. Ask viewers what happens next and what version they’d make from their own day. Those answers measure the format’s portability. Laughter and completion measure different things.

State diffusion · the screenshot case

Observed · The app displayed a stale-conversation message after two short turns. The screenshot retained both turns and the error. Sending it restored enough context to continue.

Possible mismatch · A client can submit a turn against a version or parent message that the service no longer accepts. A second surface, a retry, a late update, or an app resuming with old state can produce that result in systems that check conversation revisions. An expired session, a transient problem, or a generic error message could look similar. The screenshot cannot distinguish these explanations or show that the content caused the error.

Safe reproduction · In a disposable chat, compare a single-device baseline with ordinary actions such as app resume, reopening the chat, or advancing it on a second signed-in device before returning to the first. Use neutral text, one change at a time. Record which message was visible, which sent, and whether reopening recovered it. Repeated results would narrow the trigger. The exact server cause would still require diagnostics.

Use it in Pantheon · Carry the conversation state as well as the sentence. A handoff should hold the last confirmed point, the intended next turn, the open question, and the end state. If the destination has moved on, recover against what is current and surface conflicts before acting. Keep the source excerpt when a paraphrase might lose the vellinch.

> Last confirmed point · Optimism can make a first attempt more likely.  
> Missing turn · “And state diffusion.” “End state.”  
> Open question · What travels between people or interfaces, and what tells us it arrived?  
> End state · A reader can recover the intended next move and take it without guessing which version they inherited.